(A kitchen works out what to pour with what, lists every meal a diner
 could choose from three courses, and writes one table's ticket, each in
 one sentence.

 Every "each" clause joined by "and" adds a loop, and the leftmost is the
 outermost, so the calls come out in the order nested For each loops
 would make them. A "but if" is checked on every turn, so its condition
 can read every loop variable at once.)

To 'suggest a pairing' with a text called dish and a text called wine.
    Print "{dish} with {wine}".

A list called dishes is ["trout", "lamb"].
A list called wines is ["riesling", "claret"].

Print "What to pour with what:".
'suggest a pairing' of each dish from dishes and each wine from wines,
    but if dish is "trout" and wine is "claret" print "trout with claret, a brave choice".
Print "".

(Three clauses nest the same way as two, and so would five.)

To 'read out the meal' with a text called starter and a text called main and a text called pudding.
    Print "{starter}, then {main}, then {pudding}".

A list called starters is ["soup", "salad"].
A list called mains is ["trout", "lamb"].
A list called puddings is ["pear tart"].

Print "Every meal a diner could choose:".
'read out the meal' of each starter from starters and each main from mains and each pudding from puddings.
Print "".

(A fixed argument rides along with the clauses, the same on every call.)

To 'write the ticket' with a text called table and a text called course.
    Print "{table}: {course}".

Print "The ticket for one table:".
'write the ticket' of "table four" and each course from ["soup", "lamb", "pear tart"].
